  "comments": "<p>The standard weight of these pieces was 80 to the pound avoirdupois (1/5 ounce or 87½ grains).</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>We are indebted to Declan Magee Coins and Numista member Sjoelund for the images below indicating variety differences.<br><br><i>1911 die varieties:</i><br><br><strong>Obverse 1</strong> flat neck, D of DEI points between beads;<br><strong>Obverse 1*</strong> hollow neck, D of DEI points at a bead;<br><strong>Reverse A</strong> date close to exergue line, small beads with gap to rim;<br><strong>Reverse A*</strong> gap between date and exergue line, large beads with no gap to rim;<br><br><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/5a9ad19da0dfb.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/5a9ad19da0dfb.jpg\" alt=\"\"></a><br><br><i>1925 die varieties:</i><br><br><strong>KM#809, Sp#4056</strong> 1st portrait<br><strong>KM#824, Sp#4057</strong> Modified effigy<br><br><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/622856317f994.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/622856317f994.jpg\" alt=\"\"></a></p>",
